# AngularJS Post-Verification Checks

Run these AFTER each task that modifies AngularJS code (web/ui/js/*.js).
Reason: AngularJS has no compile-time safety for dependency injection — a missing `$inject` entry produces `undefined` at runtime with a TypeError only visible in the browser console.

## 1. $inject array validation (MANDATORY)

The #1 source of silent runtime bugs in AngularJS. When a service is injected, the function signature AND the `$inject` array must match position-by-position.

```bash
CONTROLLER_FILE="{target .js file}"

# Extract function parameters (position 1, 2, 3...)
PARAMS=$(grep -oP "(?<=function\s{0,20}\w{0,50}\s?\()[^)]+" "$CONTROLLER_FILE" \
         | head -1 | tr ',' '\n' | xargs)

# Extract $inject entries (must match params position-by-position)
INJECT=$(grep -A 50 '\$inject\s*=' "$CONTROLLER_FILE" \
         | grep -oP "'[^']+'" | tr -d "'" | head -$(echo $PARAMS | wc -w) | xargs)

# Fail loudly if mismatch
if [ "$PARAMS" != "$INJECT" ]; then
  echo "⛔ \$inject MISMATCH — MUST FIX BEFORE PROCEEDING"
  echo "  Function params: [$PARAMS]"
  echo "  \$inject array:  [$INJECT]"
  echo "  Difference: $(diff <(echo $PARAMS | tr ' ' '\n') <(echo $INJECT | tr ' ' '\n'))"
fi
```

**Rule: if counts or names differ → fix immediately. Do NOT run build with a `$inject` mismatch.** Build succeeds (no compile error) but service will be `undefined` at runtime.

## 2. Directive / controller / service registration

- New directive file? → verify registered in module's `.directive('name', fn)` call
- New service file? → verify registered in `.service()` or `.factory()` 
- New controller? → verify registered in `.controller()`
- Module file itself? → verify listed in main app module's dependency array

## 3. Template binding verification

- `ng-click`, `ng-model`, `ng-if` expressions reference actual scope/controller properties
- Interpolations `{{ vm.foo }}` bind to properties that exist
- Custom directive attributes (`sp-multi`, `sp-disabled`) match directive's scope bindings

## 4. Scope / controllerAs patterns

- If using `controllerAs: 'vm'`, template uses `vm.property` (not bare `property`)
- If using $scope directly, don't mix with vm references in same template

## 5. Canonical fetch pattern (satisfies the 5 edge case questions)

When implementing a task that fetches data and renders it, use this pattern. It satisfies all 5 UI edge case questions (null, empty, loading, error, permission) from the Surgeon kernel.

**Controller:**
```javascript
vm.items = [];               // null-safe default (Q1)
vm.loading = true;           // loading on (Q3)
vm.fetchError = false;
httpService.get('/rest/ui/{entity}')
  .then(function(data) {
    vm.items = data || [];   // null-safe (Q1 + Q2)
    vm.loading = false;      // loading off (Q3)
  })
  .catch(function(err) {
    vm.items = [];           // safe fallback (Q2)
    vm.loading = false;      // loading off (Q3)
    vm.fetchError = true;    // error state on (Q4)
    notificationService.error(ui.common.fetchError);
  });
```

**Template:**
```html
<sp-loading show="vm.loading"></sp-loading>              <!-- Q3 -->
<div ng-if="!vm.loading && !vm.fetchError">
  <sp-empty-state ng-if="vm.items.length === 0">        <!-- Q2 -->
  </sp-empty-state>
  <div ng-if="vm.items.length > 0">                     <!-- Q1 + Q2 -->
    <!-- main content -->
  </div>
</div>
<sp-error-state ng-if="vm.fetchError">                  <!-- Q4 -->
</sp-error-state>
```

**Permission (Q5):**
```javascript
vm.canSee = permissionService.hasRight('CERTIFY_ANYONE');
```
```html
<div ng-if="vm.canSee">
  <!-- feature-gated content -->
</div>
```
